# The heating curve: why a boiling pan stays at 100 °C
#
# Heat is not temperature. A vessel on a burner climbs steadily until the
# water starts to boil, and then it stops climbing — every further joule buys
# steam instead of degrees. That flat stretch is the latent heat, and it is
# the reason a pan of boiling water cannot be made hotter by turning the ring
# up, only emptier.
register lv2

# Below the plateau: 30 kJ into 100 mL is pure sensible heat.
add v1 water 100mL
heat v1 30kJ
measure v1 thermometer

# Past it. The next 30 kJ do not move the thermometer; they take water away.
new beaker
add v2 water 100mL
heat v2 60kJ
measure v2 thermometer
measure v2 balance

# And more of the same energy, same temperature, less water still.
new beaker
add v3 water 100mL
heat v3 240kJ
measure v3 thermometer
measure v3 balance

# Salt raises the plateau: the same colligative effect that lowers the
# freezing point of a salted road raises the boiling point of a salted pan.
new beaker
add v4 water 100mL
add v4 NaCl 20g
heat v4 60kJ
measure v4 thermometer

inspect v1
inspect v2
inspect v3
inspect v4

# What this does not model: the plateau's leftover heat is spread over the
# heat capacity the vessel had *before* it boiled dry, so the final
# temperature of a vessel taken past dryness is under-reported; and the
# steam leaves an open vessel rather than being followed.
